Functional Interface


A Functional Interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method.




Before Java 8, we had to create separate classes or anonymous classes to implement interfaces.


With Functional Interfaces + Lambda Expressions, we can write concise and readable code.

Benefits
	• ✅ Supports Lambda Expressions
	• ✅ Reduces boilerplate code
	• ✅ Improves readability
	• ✅ Enables Functional Programming in Java
	• ✅ Widely used in Streams API and Collections

Common Built-in Functional Interfaces

Predicate<T> // Returns boolean- filter 
Function<T,R> // Takes input, returns output - map
Consumer<T> // Takes input, returns nothing 
Consumer → forEach()
Supplier<T> // Returns value, takes no input- 
Supplier → generate()


Can a functional interface have multiple methods?
A: It can have only one abstract method, but it can have multiple default, static, and private (Java 9+) methods.


Optional.of/ofNullable/empty
of(): non-null only; 

ofNullable(): null allowed; 

empty(): empty Optional.


